<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · concept</p><h1 class="enc-title">Parinirvana</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as parinirvāṇa, parinibbāna</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a>, parinirvana is the state entered at death by someone who attained nirvana during life. It means release from samsara, karma and rebirth, and the ending of the aggregates (skandhas) that make up a being. Modern usage tends to keep &quot;nirvana&quot; for the awakening experience and &quot;parinirvana&quot; for the death. Accounts of the Buddha's parinirvana at Kuśinagara are found in the Pāli Mahāparinibbāna Sutta and its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> and Chinese parallels. Bareau judged only the death and funeral rites to be the oldest core, while some Mahāyāna scriptures, notably the Mahāyāna Mahāparinirvāṇa Sūtra, describe it as the realm of the eternal true Self of the Tathāgata.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/parinirvana.jpg" alt="Parinirvana" width="330" height="250" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Paranirvana.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>In the Buddhist view, when ordinary people die, each person's unresolved karma passes into a new birth in one of the Six Paths of samsara. The scholar Rupert Gethin explains that a person who attains nirvana does not take a new life: instead of being reborn they parinirvana, which in this sense means that the five aggregates of physical and mental phenomena that make up a being cease to occur. In Buddhist art the event is shown as a reclining Buddha, often surrounded by disciples.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Accounts of the events around the Buddha's own parinirvana appear in a wide range of canonical literature: the Pāli Mahāparinibbāna Sutta (DN 16) and its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> parallels, the Saṃyutta-nikāya, the Ekottara-āgama and other early sutras preserved in Chinese, and most of the Vinayas preserved in Chinese, such as those of the Sarvāstivādins and the Mahāsāṃghikas. Later works also describe it, among them the Sanskrit Buddhacarita, the Avadāna-śataka and the Pāli Mahāvaṃsa. The Mahāparinibbāna Sutta was first committed to writing hundreds of years after his death, yet because of its detail it has served as the main reference for most standard studies of the Buddha's life.</p><p>Bareau judged the oldest core of all these accounts to be only the parinirvana itself at Kuśinagara and the funerary rites that followed, and he held all the other detail to be later additions of little historical value. Waddell suggested that Kuśinagara lay in the region of Rampurva, north of Bettiah in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bihar">Bihar</a>, in the line of the Aśoka pillars leading from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/patna">Patna</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The Mahāyāna Mahāparinirvāṇa Sūtra, written hundreds of years later, treats the event differently. Apart from the Buddha's illness and Cunda's offering of a meal, it gives none of the day's details, and uses the occasion to set out Mahāyāna teachings such as the tathāgatagarbha or buddha-dhātu doctrine, the eternity of the Buddha and the fate of the icchantikas. It teaches that parinirvana is the realm of the Eternal, Bliss, the Self and the Pure. Paul Williams says it uses the word Self to win over non-Buddhist ascetics, though the passage he relies on comes from a part of the sutra of secondary Central Asian origin, while other parts were written in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>.</p><p>Guang Xing writes that its Mahāyāna authors asserted the eternity of the Buddha by calling him the dharmakāya and by reinterpreting his liberation as mahāparinirvāṇa with four attributes: eternity, happiness, self and purity. Michael Zimmermann notes that the Tathāgatagarbha Sūtra and the Laṅkāvatāra Sūtra also speak affirmatively of the Self.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Parinirvana” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Buddhists call his death the <b>parinirvana</b>, his final release.</blockquote><a href="/religion/buddhism">Buddhism · Origins</a></li></ul></section>
